BareProxy memory under load, by the size of the in-memory record store, 5 October 2026

Each row is one full run of live/load-test.sh (2,000 requests per second for 60 seconds over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2
and WebSocket, 20 applies), with TRACE_MEMORY set to the value in the first column, on the tree at ea04f9e (the
uncommitted change the runs show is that setting of load-test.sh itself). The
trace log went to a file in every run. Peak memory is BareProxy's VmHWM (the most resident memory it held at
any time), read from /proc when the load ends. The machine is the shared 2-CPU VM described in load-test.log;
nothing else was running.

trace-memory                   sent     failed  peak memory    CPU s result
off                         120,020          0       25 MiB     22.4 PASS
8MB                         120,020          0       46 MiB     21.5 PASS
16MB                        120,020          0       74 MiB     21.4 PASS
32MB (the old default)      120,020          0      111 MiB     21.4 PASS

What it shows
  The record store is most of BareProxy's memory under load. With it off, the peak is 25 MiB. Each MB of store
  adds about 2.6 to 3.1 MiB at the peak (computed from the rows above): the store keeps that much record JSON,
  and Go's garbage collector lets the heap grow past what is live before it runs.
  The default is now 8 MB. A full 8 MB store holds about 17,000 typical records of 493 bytes
  (TestTraceRingCapacityAtTheDefaultSize, 9.4 MB of heap), which is about 2 minutes of requests at 140 per
  second, or 8.5 seconds at 2,000 per second (computed). `status` says when its 1- and 5-minute counts
  cover less than the window. The trace log file keeps every record whatever the store's size.

